# SVI reference vs Double Heston (same-day, in-sample)

Generated by `python3 -m dh2.svi`. SVI (raw, per expiry, 5 free parameters each, no dynamics; module docstring of `dh2/svi.py`) is the
"best achievable smile fit" yardstick. The Double Heston fits are the walk-forward same-day fits (`walk_dh_main`, `walk_dhj_main`:
structural parameters from the trailing 40-day window, today's two variance levels), priced on every quote of the day.

Sample: 118 days, every 10th trading day of the test period 2022-01-01 onward (`sample_days.csv`), 1031 expiries fitted with SVI
(needs >= 6 quotes; 66 of 53,046 quotes sit in expiries too thin to fit and are excluded for ALL models in the same-quote columns).

## In-sample implied-vol RMSE (vol points, pooled over quotes)

| | SVI (5 params/expiry) | Double Heston + jumps | Double Heston, no jumps |
|---|---|---|---|
| all quotes of SVI-fitted expiries | 0.502 | 1.155 | 3.069 |
| mean of the daily RMSEs, same quotes | 0.460 | 0.994 | 2.685 |
| mean of the daily RMSEs, ALL quotes (DH as in the walk-forward) | - | 0.994 | 2.683 |

Check: the recomputed Double Heston RMSE on all quotes reproduces the stored `rmse_in` of the walk-forward files (largest difference 0.00e+00 diffusion, 0.00e+00 jumps).

